  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three broad ways organisms interact in agricultural systems, shown across both large organisms and microbes. At the top, plant–plant interactions are contrasted: facilitation, where one crop appears to support another when grown together; inhibition, where dry, cracked soil and stressed roots suggest negative effects on neighboring plants; and pre-emption, where a larger plant captures light and belowground space before smaller plants can, limiting their access to resources.\n\nThe middle imagery links these ideas to farming contexts, with examples such as livestock and crop mixtures. At the microbial level, the lower panels show corresponding mechanisms near host tissues. In facilitation, one microbe seems to consume oxygen, creating conditions that help another microbe colonize. In inhibition, microbes or host-associated defenses appear to suppress competitors, including through compounds delivered across the surface. In pre-emption, microbes occupy attachment sites or compete for a limiting nutrient, illustrated by iron sequestration, thereby preventing later arrivals from establishing.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that similar ecological interaction types—helping, harming, or getting there first—operate across scales, from crops and weeds to microbial communities associated with plants or animals.",
